<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Egypt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is the northern part of Egypt, consisting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> and the area around Memphis. Its unification with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> was the foundation of the Egyptian state, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> bore the title of ruler of both lands. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, Lower Egypt was a key administrative region, and its fragmentation contributed to the period's decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lower-egypt.jpg" alt="Lower Egypt" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Egypt_Nomes_01.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The New Kingdom emerged from the expulsion of the Hyksos, an Asiatic dynasty that had controlled <b>Lower Egypt</b> for over a century during the Second Intermediate Period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hyksos period, when chariot-mounted maryannu ruled Egypt from Avaris, Baal was identified with the Egyptian god Set and was thereafter shown wearing the crown of <b>Lower Egypt</b>, while Asherah, Astarte and Anat were given Hathor-like wigs.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the New Kingdom there were two viziers, one for Upper and one for <b>Lower Egypt</b>, who oversaw land surveys, the granaries, the treasury, building works and the archives, and sat as judges in the high court.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Den first used the title 'King of Upper and <b>Lower Egypt</b>', and expeditions reached Sinai and Nubia for copper and stone.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>The red crown and Wadjet survived as emblems of <b>Lower Egypt</b> in royal regalia and titles for three thousand years.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lower-egypt">Kingdom of Lower Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>The Thinite line culminated in Narmer, who around 3100 BCE ruled both Upper and <b>Lower Egypt</b> and is usually counted as the founder of Early Dynastic Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/proto-kingdom-of-thinis">Proto-kingdom of Thinis</a></li></ul></section>
